WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:02.805
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.805 --> 00:00:05.220
I help some high flyers get
their Bigtable performance

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.120
under control.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.220
Will their performance
be cleared for landing?

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.590
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:15.510
Flight Might provides monitoring
systems for hobby, commercial,

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.190
and experimental aircrafts.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.280
During a flight, their systems
collect hundreds of thousands

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.870
of data samples,
all of which get

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.840
pushed directly into Bigtable,
letting their development teams

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.270
view historical and
real time data about how

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.040
their systems are performing.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.140
Sadly, though, as
planes moved around,

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.870
the performance of pushing all
the sample data into the cloud

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.930
would decrease, causing
problems for the devices.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.970
The issue here is that
Bigtable, in order

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.560
to be as performance
as possible,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.560
is local to a specific region.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.440
As such, accessing Bigtable
from clients around the world

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.700
is quickly at the
mercy of latency,

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.440
which is why we recommend that
any client accessing Bigtable

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.290
be not only in the same
region, but specifically

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.610
in the same zone.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.640
To show this off, we spun
up a Bigtable load test tool

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.160
which allowed a client
in US central 1C

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.500
to connect the Bigtable
clusters in different zones

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.122
to see the performance.

00:01:06.122 --> 00:01:07.080
Now don't get me wrong.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.110
These numbers are extremely
impressive on their own,

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.240
but we can see how the
latency of distance

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.170
can quickly cause overhead for
each client push to add up.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.040
Now let's be clear.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.900
This is not a challenge
unique to Bigtable.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.550
Across all of your
cloud applications,

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.070
as the distance between your
client and server connection

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.580
point increase, so does the
latency of the transaction.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.830
Now the out of the
box solution to this

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.840
is typically simple,
which is just spin up

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.640
more clusters in more
geographic locations.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.960
But for a global company like
Flight Might, that ends up

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.140
being a lot of clusters around
the world, and the cost of that

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.910
get quickly prohibitive.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.400
After looking at a number of
options, which we describe

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.900
in more detail in
the article linked

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.358
in the description
of the video, we

00:01:47.358 --> 00:01:49.850
decided to go with a
serverless solution.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.870
Now firstly, their
clients push data

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.910
into Pub/Sub, which
allows their connections

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.730
to hit the closest Google
Cloud front end as possible.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.640
Then they have a Google Cloud
function in the same zone

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.700
as the Bigtable incidents, which
are triggered by the Pub/Sub

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.760
events and handle pushing
data right into Bigtable.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.850
With this setup, they can have
a plethora of distributed agents

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.770
submitting constant
data to Bigtable

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.464
and not have to have the
issue of connection latency.

00:02:13.464 --> 00:02:14.880
If you're interested
to know more,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.129
check out the article linked
in the description below.

00:02:17.129 --> 00:02:19.380
And don't forget to subscribe
to our YouTube cloud

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.499
channel for more great Cloud
Performance Atlas content.

00:02:22.499 --> 00:02:24.040
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.320
every millisecond counts.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.670
